se p of R times Dr  and it is equal to the absolute value  squared of the radio function times the  infinitesimal radial element  and when we only consider the distance  from the origin we need to average over  all possible angular directions so we  need to integrate over the angular parts  let's first focus on the angular  integrals  we can use the fact that y 0 0 is equal  to the constant 1 over square root of 4  pi to take its Square 1 over 4 Pi  outside followed by the integrals over  the angular coordinates  these integrals give the solid angle of  the full 3d space which is 4 pi and we  end up with one  inserting this results inside the  probability of finding the electron at a  distance R from the proton we get the  absolute value squared of the radial  function times r squared Dr  so what does all of this mean  the probability of finding the electron  at a distance R from the proton P of R  times Dr is equal to this expression  in this expression pfr is what we call  the probability density of finding the  electron at a distance R from the proton  and it is given by the square of the  radial function times r squared we can  find an explicit expression for it by  plugging in the value of the radial  function for the ground state we have at  the very top here and we get this  prefaxer times r squared times this  exponential  this expression gives the probability  density associated with finding the  electron at a distance R from the proton  irrespective of the direction and this  probability density is what we will use  next to learn more about the ground  state of the hydrogen atom  the diagram on the left shows the  probability density that we've just  derived for finding the electron at a  distance R from the proton  as a reminder in the diagram on the  right I'm showing again the radial part  of the ground state wave function which  has a maximum at the origin and decays  away from it  now at First Sight these two plots may  look inconsistent  how is it possible that given the shape  of the wave function the probability of  finding the electron at a distance R  from a proton has the shape shown on the  left with a clear maximum some distance  away from the origin  to properly understand this Behavior we  need to remember that we really have a  3D system and apart from the radial  Direction shown in these plots we also  have angular directions  this function p on the left shows the  probability of finding the electron at a  distance R from the proton irrespective  of the direction in which we look  Fab means that when we consider an  electron at a distance R from the proton  this electron could be at any point on a  sphere of radius R centered on the  proton  as we move farther away from the origin  the surface of this sphere grows and  therefore the number of positions in  which the electron can be also grows  mathematically the growth of the  spherical area as we move away from the  origin is captured by this r squared  term here  we then need to combine this purely  geometrical effect with the actual shape  of the wave function which describes an  exponential decay away from the origin  as encoded by this term  the combination of these two effects  leads to this diagram where the volume  effects dominate at low radii and the  exponential decay takes over for larger  radii with a turnover point in the  middle that bleeds to a maximum in the  probability density  to find the location of this maximum all  we need to do is to evaluate the  derivative of p and set it equal to zero  explicitly the derivative takes this  form  and we can evaluate it using the chain  Rule and we get the pre-factor the term  involving the derivative of r squared  and the term involving the derivative of  the exponential  we can take this as a common factor  multiplying by 1 minus r over a0  going back to our derivative at the top  we get this condition for the maximum  we can cancel these terms as they are  positive and we find that the maximum is  for r equal to a0  and this means that the maximum here is  located at a distance a0 from the origin  and what is the meaning of this  the most likely proton electron distance  in the ground state of the hydrogen atom  is a0 which is called the reduced bore  radius  remember that we initially introduced  the reduced bore radius in the video  where we solve the eigenvalue equation  for the hydrogen atom hamiltonian and at  that point it was a purely pragmatic  definition that helped us to simplify  the notation somewhat  we now see that that reduced ball radius  has actually a very important physical  meaning it describes the typical size of  the hydrogen atom which is given by the  most likely distance between the  electron and the proton  okay now that we've seen that the  reduced bore radius has a very important  physical meaning we should evaluate its  actual numerical value  remember the definition of a0 as equal  to this expression in terms of  fundamental physical constants and in  terms of the reduced mass of the proton  electron system  to consider its numerical value we again  need the reduced Mass mu which we said  earlier is equal to  9.104 times 10 to the minus 31 kilograms  we also need the elementary charge which  has this value the permittivity of free  space which is this  and the reduced plan constant which is  this  interesting these numerical values into  the expression at the top we find that  the reduced bore radius is equal to 5.3  times 10 to the minus 11 meters  just like we found in the case of the  ionization energy SI units are not ideal  to describe Atomic premises and we will  instead use the angstrom which is  abbreviated as a capital A with a small  circle at the top  one angstrom is defined as equal to 10  to the minus 10 meters  so with those units the reduced bore  radius is equal to  0.53 angstrom  right so that is the typical size of the  hydrogen atom  while Atomic sizes vary depending on the  element they are often compared to the  size of the hydrogen atom and therefore  described in terms of the bore radius  and for this reason this is a good  number to remember as you continue in  your study of atomic physics chemistry  and Material Science  to finish we're going to explore how the  kinetic and potential energies  separately contribute to the total  energy of the ground state of the  hydrogen atom  to simplify notation we will label the  kinetic energy term with a capital T and  the potential energy term with a capital  Phi  we start with the expectation value of  the hamiltonian in the ground state  which we can explicitly write out like  this  we can use the eigenvalue equation here  to end up with the ground state energy  as the ground state is normalized  from our earlier discussion this is  equal to the negative ionization energy  on top of that as the hamiltonian is the  sum of the kinetic and potential  energies we can also write the  expectation value of the hamiltonian as  equal to the expectation value of the  kinetic energy plus the expectation  value of the potential energy  combining these two results we get to  this relation between the expectation  values of the kinetic and potential  energies with respect to the ground  state wave function  let's next separately consider the  expectation value of the potential  energy in the ground state  spelling out the expression for the  potential energy we can rewrite the  expectation value like this  we can also take this constant factor  out  multiplying the expectation value of 1  over the magnitude of the position  operator  so our next job is to evaluate the  expectation value here which we can  first copy down  using the position representation as  spherical coordinates we can rewrite it  as the angular integrals the radial  integral and then the integrand is 1  over R times the absolute value squared  of the ground state wave function  now recall that the ground stage wave  function has no angular dependence so we  can directly evaluate the angular  integrals which give 4 pi  we can also combine these terms together  to get the integrand of the radial  integral  using the expression we got earlier for  the ground state wave function we end up  with this integrand for the radial  integral  at this point we're going to use this  standard integral which you can look up  in the usual integral tables or just  evaluate it directly  in our case P here is equal to one and  Alpha is equal to 2 over a zero so with  this we can evaluate the expectation  value of 1 over R to get the pre-factors  arising from the angular integrals and  the wave function prefactors  times the result of the standard radial  integral  finally the expression simplifies to  just 1 over a zero  the end result is really very simple the  expectation value of 1 over R in the  ground state is the inverse of the  reduced ball radius  so inserting this back to our expression  for the expectation value of the  potential energy we get this expression  let's make some room  to make progress we need to consider the  ionization energy which remember is  equal to this  we can reorganize the expression for the  ionization energy like this  and we now recognize the term in  Brackets as the inverse of the reduced  Vol radius so that we can write the  ionitation energy in terms of the  reduced or radius like this  if we now compare this expression with  this expression we can conclude that the  ionization energy is equal to minus one  half times the expectation value of the  potential energy  and finally we can write that the  expectation value of the potential  energy is equal to minus twice the  ionization energy  so let's summarize what we have just  found  first we found that the expectation  value of the hamiltonian which can be  written as the sum of the expectation  values of the kinetic energy and the  potential energy is equal to minus the  ionization energy  second we've just found that the  expectation value of the potential  energy can be written as minus two times  the ionization energy  from these results we can now easily  calculate the expectation value of the  kinetic energy  from the top equation the expectation  value of the kinetic energy is equal to  this expression  and inserting the value of the  expectation value of the hamiltonian  here and the value of the expectation  value of the potential energy here we  end up with minus e i minus minus 2  times e i which yields the ionization  energy  so what does this mean  we can write the expectation value of  the potential energy being equal to  minus twice the expectation value of the  kinetic energy  and this result shows that the magnitude  of the potential energy contribution to  the total energy is twice as large as  the contribution from the kinetic energy  for the ground state of the hydrogen  atom  this relation is actually an example of  a more General result known as the  virial theorem and I encourage you to  check out the video that we have Linked  In the description to learn more about  it  but in short the virial theorem  describes a general relation between the  expectation value of the kinetic and  potential energies when the system is in  an energy eigen State these General  relations are useful because they  provide insight into complex systems  without having to do any explicit  calculations like the ones we've done up  here  in the context of the hydrogen atom this  result will be valid not only for the  ground state but also for any excited  state  the analysis of the ground state of the  hydrogen atom has led to some very  important scales that play a key role  across Atomic physics the first one is  the ionization energy which gives the  typical energy scale associated with  atomic systems and the second one is the  bore radius which gives the typical  length scale associated with atomic  systems and that's it for today if you  liked the video please subscribe  foreign
 

